strophic  flooding destroyed more than 100  kilometers of the coquihalla highway  between hope and merit crews worked  around the clock to reopen the route  first to commercial traffic in just 35  days and today to the rest of the public  the city of iqaluit has shut down its  water treatment plant after fuel was  again discovered in the supply the city  says a breach was found in the system  last week and again today after  residents reported a fuel smell in the  tap water iqaluit was under a boil water  advisory for two months last year for  the same issue  indigenous people have long said they  are not taken seriously in canada's  healthcare system and now there is data  to back that up a study analyzing  millions of emergency room visits in  alberta found indigenous patients were  treated as less urgent even if their  complaints were the same as other  patients as heather urex west reports  advocates hope this research will help  combat systemic racism  pamela bibi has spent much of her  professional life advocating for truth  and reconciliation but in her personal  life she's had to spend a great deal of  time advocating for her family's health  care when somebody has to go to the  emergency room and get sent home right  away nobody is surprised in my family we  get asked immediately like are you on  drugs are you high why are your eyes red  like all these questions around that are  just not appropriate baby is not alone  and now new research published in the  canadian medical association journal has  found evidence that indigenous people  consistently experience discrimination  within alberta emergency rooms so some  real  hard quantitative data that systemic  racism exists that's right exactly using  data from alberta hospitals researchers  found first nations routinely receive  lower acuity triage scores important  because patients with higher scores are  given a higher priority for care what  happens  from the data that we see  is that people who are from the first  nations community are put down lower in  the list  to be seen for those same types of  injuries as people who are not from the  first nations community the alberta  research adds to a growing body of  evidence that systemic anti-indigenous  racism in health care is a national  problem we found pervasive interpersonal  systemic racism a 2020 inquiry found 84  percent of indigenous patients  experienced discrimination within bc's  health system  and of course no one can forget the  tragic death of joyce eshequan  the attica woman died in a quebec  hospital days after live streaming this  video on her chart a nurse had  identified her as a drug abuser in  withdrawal it wasn't true it makes us  very reticent to access  healthcare services and we don't do so  unless we absolutely have to whether  it's the researchers in the alberta  study the coroner's report in quebec or  the inquiry in bc the key  recommendations are the same systemic  racism within the canadian healthcare  system exists and a reckoning is overdue  whether they're nurses doctors  respiratory therapists security guards  everybody needs  to gain skills and how to  address their bias and be aware of it in  order to ensure all indigenous people  have equitable access to care heather  urex west global news calgary  more than just a number next the hockey  world honors a canadian who changed the  game  the woman known as the queen of  basketball has died lucia harris is the  only woman drafted by an nba team she's  also the first player to score in  olympic women's basketball history  sinking the first basket in the 1976  montreal games harris went on to become  the first black woman inducted into the  basketball hall of fame she's being  remembered for her charity and what she  did both on and off the court harris was  66.  another sports pioneer was honored by  the nhl last night willie o'reilly the  league's first black player had his  jersey retired by the boston bruins as  mike trelay reports even without a  lengthy nhl career his impact on hockey  can still be felt on and off the ice  [Music]  it's fair to say william's expectations  never included seeing his jersey retired  by the boston bruins maybe not even his  dreams as a young man growing up in the  1950s his sole focus was on just making  it to the nhl all i wanted was to be a  hockey player  all i needed was the opportunity  he was so overwhelmed when the bruins  called him up in 1958 he only learned he  had made history as the first black man  to play in the nhl the following day it  was the greatest thrill of my life i  believe i'll always remember this day in  the first period you look like you're a  little nervous were you yes i bet it  scared you a little bit yes it did anno  reed goes back for boston oh ree would  play 45 games over two seasons before  embarking on a successful career in the  minor leagues it was never easy  yeah i heard the racial remarks and  racial slurs and it was in chicago and  new york probably the the worst problems  i had like baseball's jackie robinson  before him he endured in relative  silence in fact he never told any of his  teams that an errant puck had left him  blind in one eye for fear they wouldn't  let him play  and while his nhl career was short 45  games in all his impact is almost  impossible to gauge countless minorities  now play hockey because of outreach  programs he helped build and former  nhler george larocque says he never  fails to give a re-credit when speaking  to children  i always talk about the history of the  game and to me it's always so important  to tell them that you know what  i know you guys looked up to me but if  it wasn't for him you wouldn't have  never seen me play sadly racism still  permeates the hockey world it's a  reality the nhl is working to change  seeing the jersey go up is a reminder to  people that it wasn't just white people  who played hockey  there are lots of other  cultures and races  that are playing hockey  and  that we need to be inclusive and that  would be a dream legacy for william rhee  microlight global news toronto  and that is global national for this  wednesday night i'm sophie louie  tonight's your canada is cavendish beach  on the north shore of prince edward  island there are beautiful spots all  over canada please email us yours to  viewers at globalnational.com  thanks for watching and have a good  night
 

